<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Greater Lebanon</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1926 CE · capital Beirut</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French-created state that enlarged Mount Lebanon with Beirut, the coast and the Beqaa, giving Lebanon its modern borders.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 1 September 1920 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-gouraud">Henri Gouraud</a> proclaimed the State of Greater <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lebanese-republic">Lebanon</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beirut">Beirut</a>. Responding to the wishes of the Maronite Patriarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elias-peter-hoayek">Elias Hoayek</a> and others, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a> to the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mount-lebanon-mutasarrifate">Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state was administered by French governors under the high commissioner, with an elected representative council from 1922 organised on confessional lines. The enlargement made it economically viable but brought in large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia populations, many of whom wanted union with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beirut">Beirut</a> became the seat of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-mandate-for-syria-and-the-lebanon">French Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In May 1926 a constitution was adopted that turned the state into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-debbas">Charles Debbas</a> as its first president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/greater-lebanon.jpg" alt="Greater Lebanon" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ch1902 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lebanon_during_French_Mandate_(1920-1943).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1926 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Beirut</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henri Gouraud</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maronite Christianity, Sunni and Shia Islam, Druze</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>State of Greater Lebanon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1923&amp;focus=greater-lebanon" aria-label="Greater Lebanon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5585 949 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Greater Lebanon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5905 1205L5912 1207M5905 1205L5905 1205M5905 1205L5905 1205"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5912 1207h0M5905 1205h0M5905 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5905 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5905 1205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Beirut. <a href="/atlas?year=1923&amp;focus=greater-lebanon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
